A1. A hook-up with a loudspeaker. ~ WEDDING
A7. People are always acting up here. ~ THEATRE
A14. The generic name of various microscopic Protozoa. (How do you like that one?) ~ AMOEBA
A16. This often gets stewed in restaurants. ~ OYSTER
A17. Rabid Bohemians (init.) ~ RE
A18. Hot air escapes through these. ~ WINDPIPES
A21. Unit of measurement. ~ EM
A22. This is a darling. ~ PET
A24. What the villain did in the good old plays. ~ SNEERED
A25. The Football Blues—played at Princeton. ~ ELI
A26. Fat people use up a lot of it—and not for reducing either. ~ AREA
A28. These little boys went to war. ~ YANKS
A29. A garden famous for its apples. ~ EDEN
A30. Rulers that had two feet (the Bolshevists broke them in Russia). ~ TSARS
A32. French article (plural). ~ LES
A33. A good way to get home if you're a little late. ~ SLIDE
A34. These are red at Coney Island. ~ HOTS
A36. A good way to reduce the weight of all flesh. ~ DIET
A37. Nymphs. (And heavenly ones, according to the Mohammedans.) ~ HOURIS
A38. Shoutings. ~ SALVOS
A40. Noah says, "One who uses." ~ USER
A42. Webster says, "One who does." ~ DOER
A44. What to do with a JUDGE subscription (adv.) ~ RENEW
A46. The American Sphinx. ~ CAL
A48. Girls of to-day (according to Granny). ~ SNIPS
A50. Called blooming bounders in England. ~ CADS
A51. Professional slang. ~ ARGOT
A53. Often found hanging around the girls. ~ SASH
A54. An indefinite length of time (over 119 minutes—abbr.). ~ HRS
A55. What baby does to earn his groceries. ~ CLAMORS
A57. Very green in the spring. ~ LEA
A58. A little word with a big meaning. ~ IF
A59. Where a student lands when he slips at school. ~ PROBATION
A61. Expression meaning most anything. ~ UM
A62. A dark man in Africa. ~ NUBIAN
A64. Fair in love and war (ask any gentleman). ~ BLONDE
A66. Try and find one in a Pullman. ~ SLEEPER
A67. Tall timber. ~ REDWOOD

D1. Sitting Bull's favorite highway. ~ WARPATH
D2. Hot stuff—when coaled. ~ EMEERS
D3. Execute. ~ DO
D4. All wet. ~ DEW
D5. Wading bird related to herons. ~ IBIS
D6. Billy Goat's girl friend. ~ NANNY
D8. They say we have these as long as we live. ~ HOPES
D9. What flappers get when the wind is blowing. ~ EYED
D10. There's a kick to this. ~ ASS
D11. Tough Town (init.)—probably Chicago. ~ TT
D12. What drunks did before Prohibition—and what they do now. ~ REELED
D13. Fur bearing animals. ~ ERMINES
D15. Condition of the eyes when the lid is lifted. ~ OPENED
D19. The great divide in the poker game. ~ DEAL
D20. Annoys. ~ IRKS
D23. Parlor athletes. ~ TEAHOUNDS
D25. A whole page in JUDGE. ~ EDITORIAL
D27. Excites into action. ~ AROUSES
D29. Indispensable to every football game. ~ ELEVENS
D31. To scatter. ~ STREW
D33. Where fodder goes for the winter. ~ SILOS
D35. If she says this she's a lady. ~ SIR
D36. The kin you love to touch. ~ DAD
D39. Fuller's kids. ~ URCHINS
D41. A collector of antiques. ~ RAGMAN
D43. How granny feels about the Younger Generation. ~ ASHAMED
D45. What you get if you "listen-in." ~ EARFUL
D46. This is good in a pinch. ~ CRAB
D47. Plunder. ~ LOOT
D49. This is false. ~ PSEUDO
D51. There just couldn't be anyone else around when you're like this. ~ ALONE
D52. A distinct community of Indians. ~ TRIBE
D55. African golf. ~ CRAP
D56. Betrayed. ~ SOLD
D59. This has a lot of crust. ~ PIE
D60. A good time to subscribe to JUDGE (adv.). ~ NOW
D63. To have existence. ~ BE
D65. This is a refusal. ~ NO


